`_handle_dot_call` asked whether the project plausibly owned the written
module -- as a SUBSTRING test, `any(f"{module}." in k for k in
global_symbols)`, though the comment above it described a prefix-COMPONENT
test -- and then let `resolver.lookup(func_name, path_hint=...)` bind by BARE
NAME. On phoenix-framework that produced 202 false edges: `Logger.error(...)`
landing on an `error/1` inside an EEx GENERATOR TEMPLATE, `Path.join(...)` on
a Phoenix Channel's `join/3` in another template, `Mix.raise(...)` on a test
controller's `raise/1`, and `Logger.info(...)` on a **defp**, which Elixir
does not permit to be called from another module at all.

TWO SUB-MECHANISMS, instrumented on the live run rather than taken from the
filing, and the second is why a component-aware fix would not have been
enough:
  SUFFIX ARTIFACT, matching mid-component -- `Logger.` inside
  `Phoenix.Logger.install`, `Path.` inside `...StaticPath.path`,
  `Supervisor.` inside `Phoenix.Endpoint.Supervisor.init`.
  NAMESPACE PREFIX, a real component-boundary match that is still wrong --
  phoenix defines 56 modules under `Mix.`, so `Mix.raise/1` passes any
  component test.

THE FIX IS NOT THE ONE PRE-REGISTERED, AND THE MEASUREMENT IS WHY. Tightening
the gate to an EXACT module-ownership question was built and measured first.
It is necessary and NOT sufficient: with the gate exact, every edge the
bare-name lookup still produced corpus-wide was STILL wrong --
`Phx.New.Umbrella.render()` to a template's `render`, `Router.call()` to an
unrelated test helper's `call`. The gate decides WHICH modules may pass; the
defect is the bare-name bind itself. So it is removed, and the exact-ownership
helper written two hours earlier is deleted as dead code. A qualified call now
resolves only by EXACT name -- `Module.func`, or through alias expansion --
and anything else takes the unresolved edge, which names the right module and
lets the catalogue match.

TWO PREREQUISITES THE OLD SUBSTRING TEST HAD BEEN COVERING FOR, each of which
lost true edges the moment it stopped:
  * `alias Phx.New.{Project}`, the MULTI-ALIAS BRACE FORM, registered NO hint
    at all -- its tree is `arguments -> dot -> [alias, ".", tuple]`, so there
    is no `alias` child of `arguments` to find. 71 true first-party edges.
  * An alias binds its FIRST COMPONENT, not the whole name:
    `alias Mix.Tasks.Phx.Gen` makes `Gen.Context.build(...)` mean
    `Mix.Tasks.Phx.Gen.Context.build/2`. 29 more.
The pre-registered control caught both -- the first pass lost 300 first-party
edges, 29 of them real -- and it held only after both were fixed.

